% ============================================================================
% syllable-blending — combining separate syllables to say a word, a
% THIRTEENTH literacy sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib,
% LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% The exact OPPOSITE direction from the already-shipped
% `syllable-segmentation.adj` (which decomposes a word into its two
% syllables): a new `syllable_blending(syllable_one, syllable_two, word)`
% table names the word formed by BLENDING two separate syllables together —
% the same "opposite direction, separate table" shape already proven by
% `phoneme-blending.adj` versus `phoneme-segmentation.adj`. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? syllable_blending(lap, top, $Word)   % laptop
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a THREE-column table like
% `phoneme-blending.adj`): the one `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `syllable_blending(syllable_one, syllable_two, word)` carrying the
% Reading Rockets citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the blended word WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on any syllable pair not the one the
% cited page walks through. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind the word and
% recall the two syllables that blend into it:
%
%     ? syllable_blending($S1, $S2, laptop)   % lap, top
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module — the SAME already-vetted page
% `syllable-count.adj`/`syllable-segmentation.adj`/`phoneme-blending.adj`/
% `phoneme-segmentation.adj` all already cite, a DIFFERENT section of it
% (its "Blending syllables" demonstration, distinct from the "Segmenting
% syllables" section `syllable-segmentation.adj` cites) — WebFetch-verified
% THREE separate times for consistency before writing, all byte-identical.
% The exact span (naming both syllables and the word they blend into):
%
%   "I can say each syllable in a word and then I can blend the syllables to
%    say the word. As I say each syllable, I will lay down a card. I will
%    lay the cards left to right. Watch me. I say each syllable and put down
%    a card: 'lap' [place a card] 'top' [place a card so it appears
%    left-to-right for students]. Now I sweep my finger below the cards and
%    say the whole word: 'laptop' [sweep finger below the cards
%    left-to-right]."
%
% `trust consensus` — the same tier this page's sibling sections already
% earned. Deliberately scoped to ONLY this ONE blend the cited page walks
% through step by step — honestly narrow (mirroring `phoneme-blending.adj`'s
% precedent of one clean row) rather than inventing a blend the source does
% not demonstrate. This same page also has an "Adding syllables" section
% (also "lap" + "top" -> "laptop") that appears to name the SAME syllable
% pair under a different heading, not a distinct arity like the
% phoneme-blending/phoneme-addition split — deliberately NOT shipped as a
% second table to avoid a near-duplicate; if a future WebFetch turns up a
% genuinely distinct "Adding syllables" example, it can be revisited then.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table syllable_blending {
    columns syllable_one, syllable_two, word

    row (lap, top, laptop)

    source "I can say each syllable in a word and then I can blend the syllables to say the word. As I say each syllable, I will lay down a card. I will lay the cards left to right. Watch me. I say each syllable and put down a card: 'lap' [place a card] 'top' [place a card so it appears left-to-right for students]. Now I sweep my finger below the cards and say the whole word: 'laptop' [sweep finger below the cards left-to-right]."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
